<body>
<h1>Unit 21: Potential &amp; Potential Energy</h1><p class="meta">Physics XI  |  NEB (Grade 11)  |  Phy. 101 &nbsp;•&nbsp; Content area: Electric &amp; Magnetism</p><p class="summary">Potential is the scalar alternative to the vector field. Because only differences are measurable, the zero of potential is a convention - and because the field is conservative, path never enters the answer.</p><h2>What the model asserts</h2><ol><li>Electric potential is energy per unit charge, V = U/q, and a potential difference is the work done per unit charge by the field.</li><li>For a point charge V = kq/r with V tending to zero at infinity, and the potential difference is dV = -E.dl along any path.</li><li>Only potential differences are physically meaningful; the choice of zero is a convention, and it changes no experiment.</li><li>The potential energy of a system of point charges is the sum of pairwise terms q1 q2/(4 pi epsilon0 r_ij); the self-energy of a continuous distribution requires a self-consistent calculation.</li><li>Equipotential surfaces are perpendicular to field lines, and the work in moving a charge between two points on the same equipotential is zero.</li><li>Potential is a scalar, which is why circuit and field problems are often shorter in potential language than in field language.</li><li>Potential is defined only up to an additive constant, so only differences are physical; a conductor is one equipotential surface, and the field is minus the gradient of the potential.</li></ol><h2>Conditions, assumptions and limits</h2><ul><li>The 1/r potential law assumes a point charge in vacuum; for a conductor the entire surface is one equipotential at a single value.</li><li>Potential differences are path-independent, so only the endpoints matter - but a potential difference between two points is not simply E.d unless the path is straight and along the field.</li><li>Moving a charge changes the stored energy of the whole system, and the work is shared between the field and the agent that moves it.</li><li>In a medium with permittivity epsilon the potential of a charge is reduced by the factor epsilon_r; the free charge does not change, the field does.</li><li>The expression U = qV holds for a conservative, time-independent field; in a time-varying field the work done on the charge also changes the energy stored in the field itself.</li></ul><h2>Key numbers</h2><table><tr><th>Quantity</th><th>Value</th></tr><tr><td>Potential of one volt</td><td>1 joule per coulomb</td></tr><tr><td>Field between the plates of a charged parallel-plate capacitor</td><td>sigma/epsilon0</td></tr><tr><td>Potential of the Earth's surface relative to infinity</td><td>about -1.0e5 V</td></tr><tr><td>Energy gained by an electron accelerated through 100 V</td><td>about 1.6e-17 J (100 eV)</td></tr></table><h2>Open questions and mysteries</h2><div class="mystery"><p><b>How much energy is stored in an electromagnetic field, and how is it distributed in space?</b></p><p class="status">Under active scrutiny</p><p>Classically the energy density is (1/2) epsilon E^2 plus a magnetic term; the quantum description in terms of photons remains less intuitive.</p></div><div class="mystery"><p><b>Can energy be extracted usefully from the vacuum?</b></p><p class="status">Frontier</p><p>Casimir and related effects are real and measurable but tiny; extracting macroscopic energy from vacuum fluctuations is not established.</p></div><h2>Inquiry prompts</h2><ol><li>Compute the potential on the axis of a uniformly charged disc and identify its far-field limit.</li><li>Show in one dimension that E = -dV/dx, using a finite-difference argument rather than calculus.</li><li>Compare the energy stored in a charged capacitor with the work required to charge it, and explain why they are equal.</li></ol><h2>Further reading</h2><ul><li><a href="https://openstax.org/books/university-physics-volume-2/pages/index">OpenStax: University Physics Volume 2</a></li><li><a href="https://openstax.org/books/university-physics-volume-2/pages/7-1-electric-potential-energy">OpenStax: Electric Potential Energy</a></li><li><a href="https://openstax.org/books/university-physics-volume-2/pages/7-2-electric-potential-and-potential-difference">OpenStax: Electric Potential and Potential Difference</a></li><li><a href="https://openstax.org/books/university-physics-volume-2/pages/7-5-equipotential-surfaces-and-conductors">OpenStax: Equipotential Surfaces and Conductors</a></li></ul><p class="note">Physics XI — Academic Companion — a companion brief, not a replacement for the prescribed NEB text. The open questions describe live research frontiers; they are enrichment, not a claim that the established school-level model is invalid. Check the prescribed text and syllabus for examinable scope.</p>
</body>
